(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-10"] = {"14.1":{"s":"Semiconductors are materials whose electrical properties lie between those of conductors and insulators, and can be tailored by adding impurities. Almost every electronic gadget, from a mobile phone to a satellite, depends on them. This chapter covers how solids conduct, intrinsic and extrinsic semiconductors, the p-n junction diode and its uses, and the thermistor.","x":["Semiconductors are materials whose electrical conductivity lies between that of a conductor and that of an insulator. The two most common ones are silicon and germanium. Their special feature is that their conductivity can be changed a great deal, by adding a very small quantity of an impurity, by a change of temperature, or by light.","Almost every electronic device, from a mobile phone to a satellite, uses semiconductor devices, such as diodes, transistors and integrated circuits. This chapter covers how solids conduct, intrinsic and extrinsic semiconductors, the p-n junction diode and its uses, some other devices, and the thermistor.","Study tip: be clear about the difference between the majority carriers and the minority carriers, and between forward bias and reverse bias. Most questions in this chapter are about these two ideas."],"e":"A pure silicon crystal at room temperature conducts only a little. If one atom in a million is replaced by a phosphorus atom, its conductivity rises by a factor of thousands."},"14.2":{"s":"Solids are conductors, insulators or semiconductors according to how many free carriers they have. In a solid the energy levels of the atoms spread into bands. The valence band is the top filled band and the conduction band is the empty one above it; the gap between them is the energy gap. In a metal the bands overlap. In an insulator the gap is large (diamond 5.47 eV). In a semiconductor it is small (silicon 1.12 eV, germanium 0.66 eV).","f":[{"x":"1 eV = 1.6*10^-19 J","n":"The electron-volt: the energy gained by an electron moving through a potential difference of one volt.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 14.2, p.245"}],"w":["The energy gap of a semiconductor is small enough for heat to lift a few electrons across it; an insulator's gap is not."],"x":["In an isolated atom, the electrons have fixed energy levels. In a solid, the levels of the many atoms spread into bands. The valence band is the highest band that is filled with electrons, and the conduction band is the empty band above it. The gap between them is the energy gap, E_g. Electrons can carry a current only if they can move into empty energy states, in the conduction band.","In a conductor (a metal), the valence band and the conduction band overlap, or the valence band is only partly filled, so there are always many free electrons. In an insulator, the energy gap is large, for example 5.47 eV for diamond, so no electrons can cross at room temperature. In a semiconductor, the gap is small, for example 1.12 eV for silicon and 0.66 eV for germanium. At room temperature, heat lifts a few electrons across the gap.","The energy is measured in electron volts: 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. A photon can lift an electron across the gap if its energy is at least E_g, that is, if its wavelength is at most hc/E_g. For silicon, this is about 1110 nm.","Common mistake: thinking that a semiconductor has no free electrons. It has a few at room temperature, and none at absolute zero."],"e":"Silicon: E_g = 1.12 eV, so the longest wavelength that can lift an electron is 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸/(1.12 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 1.11 × 10⁻⁶ m, which is in the infrared."},"14.4":{"s":"A pure semiconductor, such as silicon or germanium, is called intrinsic. At absolute zero all its valence electrons are in covalent bonds and it does not conduct. At room temperature heat breaks a few bonds. Each broken bond frees an electron and leaves a hole, which behaves like a positive charge. Both electrons and holes carry current, and their numbers are equal.","f":[{"x":"n_e = n_h","n":"Intrinsic semiconductor: the number density of free electrons equals that of holes.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 14.4, p.246"}],"w":["A hole is the absence of an electron in a bond; the current in the same direction is carried by holes moving one way and electrons the other."],"x":["A pure semiconductor, such as silicon or germanium, with no impurity, is called an intrinsic semiconductor. Each atom has four valence electrons, and it forms four covalent bonds with its neighbours. At absolute zero, all the electrons are in bonds, there are no free carriers, and the crystal is an insulator.","At room temperature, heat energy breaks a few bonds. Each broken bond frees an electron, which can move through the crystal, and leaves a hole, which is a missing electron in a bond. A hole behaves like a positive charge. A neighbouring electron can move into the hole, and then the hole is at a new place. So the hole moves in the opposite direction from the electron. Both the electrons and the holes carry the current. In an intrinsic semiconductor, n_e = n_h = n_i.","When the temperature rises, n_i rises fast, so the conductivity rises, and the resistance falls. This is the opposite of the behaviour of a metal. A semiconductor has a negative temperature coefficient of resistance.","Common mistake: thinking that a hole is a particle that exists on its own. It is the absence of an electron in a bond, and it acts like a positive charge, +e."],"e":"Pure silicon at 300 K has about 1.5 × 10¹⁶ free electrons per m³, and the same number of holes. In a metal, there are about 10²⁸ free electrons per m³, which is why a metal conducts so much better."},"14.5":{"s":"Adding a small amount of impurity (doping) greatly increases the conductivity of a semiconductor. A pentavalent dopant such as phosphorus, arsenic or antimony gives an n-type semiconductor, where electrons are the majority carriers. A trivalent dopant such as boron, aluminium or indium gives a p-type semiconductor, where holes are the majority carriers. Both types are electrically neutral, even though they have more carriers of one kind.","f":[{"x":"n_e >> n_h (n-type), n_h >> n_e (p-type)","n":"Majority carriers in doped semiconductors: electrons in n-type, holes in p-type.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 14.5, p.247"}],"w":["An n-type crystal is not negatively charged: the donor atoms left behind carry the positive charge that balances the extra electrons."],"x":["Doping is the addition of a small amount of an impurity to a pure semiconductor, in order to increase the conductivity. A semiconductor with an impurity is called an extrinsic semiconductor. A very small amount, such as one atom in a million, is enough to raise the conductivity by a large factor.","An n-type semiconductor is made by doping with a pentavalent atom, such as phosphorus, arsenic or antimony. It has five valence electrons. Four make bonds, and the fifth is almost free, so the dopant is called a donor. The majority carriers are electrons (n_e is much larger than n_h), and the minority carriers are holes. A p-type semiconductor is made by doping with a trivalent atom, such as boron, aluminium or indium. It has only three valence electrons, so it leaves a hole in a bond. It is called an acceptor. The majority carriers are holes (n_h is much larger than n_e).","In every semiconductor, in equilibrium, n_e × n_h = n_i². So if the electrons are increased by doping, the holes decrease.","An extrinsic semiconductor is electrically neutral. The extra electrons of an n-type crystal are balanced by the positive donor ions, which are fixed in the crystal. Common mistake: saying that an n-type crystal is negatively charged."],"e":"Silicon with n_i = 1.5 × 10¹⁶ per m³ is doped with phosphorus so that n_e = 5 × 10²² per m³. Then n_h = n_i²/n_e = 4.5 × 10⁹ per m³. The holes are the minority carriers."},"14.7":{"s":"A p-n junction is a p-type and an n-type crystal joined. At the junction electrons and holes diffuse across, leaving a depletion region with no free carriers and a built-in potential barrier. In forward bias (p to the positive terminal) the barrier is lowered and a current of milliamperes flows. In reverse bias the barrier grows and only a tiny current flows. So a diode conducts in one direction only.","f":[{"x":"R_d = V/I","n":"Static (DC) resistance of a diode at a point of its I-V curve.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 14.7, p.254"},{"x":"r_d = delta V/delta I","n":"Dynamic (AC) resistance: the reciprocal of the slope of the I-V curve at that point.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 14.7, p.254"}],"w":["An ideal diode has zero resistance in forward bias and infinite resistance in reverse bias.","Forward bias lowers the potential barrier and narrows the depletion region; reverse bias does the opposite."],"x":["A p-n junction is formed when a p-type crystal and an n-type crystal are joined. Near the junction, electrons from the n side diffuse into the p side, and holes from the p side diffuse into the n side. They recombine. This leaves a thin region, the depletion region, which has no free carriers, and has fixed ions: positive donors on the n side and negative acceptors on the p side. These ions create an electric field and a potential barrier, which stops further diffusion. Its size is 0.7 V in silicon, and 0.3 V in germanium.","In forward bias, the p side is joined to the positive terminal. The applied voltage opposes the barrier, so the barrier is lowered, the depletion region is narrower, and a current flows. The current is very small until the voltage reaches the knee voltage, and then it rises quickly. In reverse bias, the p side is joined to the negative terminal. The barrier becomes higher, the depletion region wider, and only a tiny leakage current (microamperes), caused by the minority carriers, flows.","So a diode conducts in one direction only. An ideal diode has zero resistance in forward bias, and infinite resistance in reverse bias. For a real diode, the static resistance is R = V/I, and the dynamic resistance is r = ΔV/ΔI at the working point.","Common mistake: mixing the two biases. Forward bias: p to the positive terminal. Reverse bias: p to the negative terminal."],"e":"A silicon diode in series with a resistor of 430 Ω and a battery of 5.0 V, in forward bias, has a voltage drop of 0.70 V. The current is (5.0 − 0.70)/430 = 10 mA. If the battery is reversed, the current is almost zero."},"14.8":{"s":"Semiconductor devices are small and light, need little power and are fast, because they have no filament that must heat up and they work at low voltage. They are also thermally stable and easy to make into integrated circuits. Their disadvantages are that they are sensitive to temperature and radiation, cannot handle high power, are affected by electrostatic charge, and need controlled conditions to manufacture.","x":["Semiconductor devices have replaced the old vacuum tubes, because they have several advantages. They are small and light. They need little power, and they work at a low voltage. They have no filament that must be heated, so they work at once, with no warm-up time. They are fast, do not wear out easily, are thermally stable over a wide range, and can be made in a very large number on one small chip, as an integrated circuit.","They also have some disadvantages. Their properties depend on the temperature, and they are damaged by high temperatures. They cannot handle very high power or very high voltage. They are sensitive to radiation and to electrostatic charge, and so need care in handling. Their manufacture needs very clean conditions and a high skill.","For this reason, for very high power uses, such as large radio transmitters, special tubes and devices are still used. But in nearly all other cases, semiconductors are used.","Common mistake: thinking that semiconductor devices have no disadvantage. Remember their sensitivity to temperature and their limit on power."],"e":"An old radio with tubes took a minute to warm up and used 50 W. A modern radio with transistors works at once and may use less than 1 W from a small battery."},"14.9":{"s":"Semiconductor devices have many uses. A solar cell turns light into electricity. A photo resistor changes resistance with light. A photodiode conducts when illuminated. A transistor amplifies and switches. LEDs and solid state lasers emit light with little power. Integrated circuits hold thousands of diodes and transistors on one chip and work in almost all electronic devices.","x":["Semiconductor devices are used everywhere. A p-n junction diode is used as a rectifier, to change AC to DC. A solar cell converts light energy into electrical energy, from the junction that is lit by sunlight. A photodiode, which works in reverse bias, conducts more when light falls on it, so it is used as a light detector. A photoresistor changes its resistance with the light on it.","A light emitting diode (LED) is a p-n junction that gives out light, when it is forward biased, because of the recombination of electrons and holes. The colour depends on the energy gap of the material. LEDs use little power and last for a long time. Solid state lasers are used in many devices, such as in a CD or DVD player, a barcode reader and in communication by optical fibres.","A transistor is used as an amplifier and as a switch. An integrated circuit (IC) has thousands or millions of transistors, diodes and resistors on a small chip of silicon. It is the basic part of computers, mobile phones, televisions and almost all electronic devices.","Common mistake: using a photodiode and an LED the wrong way round. A photodiode turns light into current, and an LED turns current into light."],"e":"A remote control for a TV has an LED that gives out infrared light in pulses. The TV has a photodiode that receives the pulses, and a circuit that reads the signals in them."},"14.10":{"s":"A thermistor is a temperature-sensitive resistor made from a metal-oxide semiconductor, whose resistance changes strongly with temperature. In a negative temperature coefficient (NTC) thermistor the resistance falls as the temperature rises; in a positive coefficient (PTC) thermistor it rises. Thermistors can measure very small temperature changes and are used in temperature sensors, and PTC types as resettable fuses.","w":["NTC thermistor: resistance decreases when heated. PTC thermistor: resistance increases when heated."],"x":["A thermistor is a resistor whose resistance changes a great deal with temperature. It is made from a mixture of metal oxides, such as the oxides of manganese, nickel and cobalt, which behave as semiconductors. A thermistor is small, quick to respond, and can measure very small changes of temperature.","There are two kinds. In a negative temperature coefficient (NTC) thermistor, the resistance falls when the temperature rises, because more charge carriers are freed by the heat. In a positive temperature coefficient (PTC) thermistor, the resistance rises when the temperature rises, and it rises sharply above a certain temperature. A thermistor of the NTC kind has the resistance R = R₀ exp[β(1/T − 1/T₀)], where β is a constant of the material.","Uses: NTC thermistors are used in digital thermometers, in temperature sensors for ovens, refrigerators and car engines, and to limit the current when a device is first switched on. PTC thermistors are used as resettable fuses, since a large current heats them, and the rising resistance cuts the current. They are also used in the heaters of some machines, which keep a steady temperature.","Common mistake: thinking that all thermistors behave in the same way. NTC: R falls when T rises. PTC: R rises when T rises."],"e":"A thermistor with β = 3000 K has a resistance of 10.0 kΩ at 298 K (25 °C). At 308 K (35 °C), its resistance is 7.2 kΩ. A circuit can use this change to measure temperature to a fraction of a degree."}};
